so far in this series you've queried  data stored within bigquery by following  a common path of ingesting your data  storing it and then analyzing it but  it's also possible to query data stored  externally or outside of bigquery  in today's episode of bigquery spotlight  we're diving in  to these external data sources  [Music]  wait so the world exists outside of  bigquery  of course it does the good news is that  it's possible to leave your data in  place  and use bigquery as your query engine we  call these sources external or federated  data sources  this functionality is currently  supported for data residing in google  drive  cloud storage cloud sql and bigtable  before we look closer at how to query  these sources  let's discuss a few notable differences  you'll experience with external data  sources  first to note is that the query  performance for external data sources  may not equal the query performance of  data stored in bigquery  so if query speed is a priority in your  case you may want to load the data  directly into bigquery  secondly when querying an external  source bigquery cannot predict the  amount of data to be processed  so you will only know after you run your  query finally  the results are not cached like they  would be when querying data stored in  bigquery  caching is a great way to save costs and  improve performance on repeated queries  when the underlying data hasn't changed  so given these considerations what are  some popular use cases for external data  sources  overall this feature is best for  short-term less frequently accessed data  for example you could use external data  sources to support loading and  transforming your data in just a single  pass  in this workflow you query the external  source transform the data as part of the  query  and then write the results as a  permanent table in bigquery storage  another use case is for joining small  amounts of frequently changing data with  data stored in bigquery  by keeping the frequently changing data  as an external data source  it does not need to be reloaded into  bigquery every time it is updated  an example here is querying data that  lives in a google sheet  even as the sheet is edited in real time  you can run  queries over the data and the results  will reflect the live  up-to-date information okay so now let's  look at the how for setting up external  data sources  in bigquery you can follow along in your  own bigquery sandbox  if you don't have one yet set one up  following the video link below  in this example you'll run a query over  a collection of json files located in a  cloud storage bucket  these files have the flight performance  data for all domestic flights in the  united states from 2014  starting in the console create a new  data set highlight your project name in  the left-hand nav  and click create data set name the data  set flight underscore performance  choose us for the location and click  create data set  now highlight your new data set and  click create table  under source choose google cloud storage  quick note if your data was located in  google drive  such as in a google sheet or in bigtable  you would choose those alternate sources  in the dropdown  the flight performance data is located  in a public bucket which any gcp user  can access with the uri  address paste the uri provided in the  description below into the gcs bucket  field  you'll see there's a wildcard character  in the uri which indicates to bigquery  to include all the json files that  adhere to the specific naming convention  next under destination make sure you set  the table type to external  name the table 2014  and you can auto detect the schema in  this case  finally click create table since there  is no data ingestion involved you'll see  the table immediately populate under the  data set  look at the table details where you can  see the external configuration that you  just set up  and a table size of zero bytes because  your external table does not use any  bigquery storage  now you can run a query that references  the external table  in our query we're selecting account of  all flights by carrier as we discussed  earlier you cannot see the amount of  data processed until after the query  completes  in this example you created a permanent  table for the external data source  however you can also query an external  source using a temporary table  which is useful for one-time ad-hoc  queries or for etl processes  more information on how to create and  query temporary external tables is  linked below  want to analyze data in cloud sql  creating the initial connection follows  a different process than you just saw  for cloud storage and drive  but never fear we've created a quick  start video linked below that shows you  end to end how to connect to and  query your mysql or postgres instance in  cloud sql  looking to data sources beyond the  google cloud ecosystem  we recently announced bigquery omni  which gives bigquery users the ability  to analyze data stored in other public  clouds  be sure to check out the announcement  blog post link below  thanks for joining us today for bigquery  spotlight watch out for our next episode  and remember  stay curious  [Music]  you
 

